Issues found
============

1. Sensitive data is written to the console  
   • The full primary-account number (PAN) is printed. This violates PCI-DSS and may end up in log files or browser dev-tools.  
   • The message may be captured by client-side error-reporting or analytics.

2. Clear-text transport (`http://`)  
   • Card data is sent over an unencrypted connection → trivial to intercept or modify (MITM).  
   • Most browsers block mixed-content requests when the page itself is HTTPS.

3. No input validation  
   • `cardNumber` is accepted as-is. You should at least run a Luhn check and length check.  
   • `amount` is not checked for being a positive number with the required precision.

4. Missing request headers  
   • No `Content-Type: application/json`; some servers will treat the body as `text/plain`.  
   • No authentication/authorization header (e.g. bearer token, HMAC signature) → anyone who can reach the endpoint could attempt charges.

5. No error/timeout handling  
   • Network errors throw; they are not caught → unhandled-rejection crash.  
   • If the server hangs the promise never settles; user waits forever.

6. Poor return contract  
   • `response.ok` is only `true/false` on HTTP status 200-299. Business failures returned as 200 with `{error:"…"}`
     will be reported as “success”.  
   • No propagation of additional data (transactionId, decline code, etc.).

7. Hard-coded endpoint  
   • Makes testing/staging difficult and leaks internal topology.

8. Potential memory exposure  
   • The raw PAN stays in memory for the lifetime of the function and any closure that captures it.

Fixes & improvements
--------------------

```js
import { AbortController } from 'abort-controller';          // node < 18
import validate from './validateCard.js';                    // your own helpers
import { maskPan, secureErase } from './pciUtils.js';
import logger from './logger.js';

const PAYMENT_URL  = process.env.PAYMENT_URL ?? 'https://payment-api.internal/charge';
const API_KEY      = process.env.PAYMENT_KEY;                // rotate regularly
const FETCH_TIMEOUT = 5_000;                                 // ms

export async function processPayment(cardNumber, amount) {
  /* 1. Validate user input */
  validate.cardNumber(cardNumber);         // Luhn, length, issuer rules
  validate.amount(amount);                 // positive, two decimals, limits

  /* 2. Log ONLY non-sensitive info */
  logger.info(`Processing payment: card=**** **** **** ${cardNumber.slice(-4)}, amount=${amount}`);

  /* 3. Prepare abort controller for timeout */
  const ac        = new AbortController();
  const id        = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), FETCH_TIMEOUT);

  try {
    /* 4. Perform HTTPS request with auth and correct headers */
    const res = await fetch(PAYMENT_URL, {
      method : 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type' : 'application/json',
        'Authorization': `Bearer ${API_KEY}`,
      },
      body   : JSON.stringify({ card: cardNumber, amount }),
      signal : ac.signal,
    });

    /* 5. Handle non-2xx statuses explicitly */
    if (!res.ok) {
      const text = await res.text();
      throw new Error(`Payment API ${res.status} – ${text}`);
    }

    /* 6. Parse body; expect JSON */
    const data = await res.json();                    // { transactionId, status, ... }
    return { success: true, ...data };

  } catch (err) {
    logger.error('Payment failed', err);
    return { success: false, message: err.message };
  } finally {
    /* 7. Clear timeout and wipe sensitive data */
    clearTimeout(id);
    secureErase(cardNumber);                          // overwrite buffer if possible
  }
}
```

Additional recommendations
--------------------------

• Tokenize on the client: never handle raw PAN in front-end code; use Stripe-like elements or transient tokens.  
• Enforce HTTPS on the page itself with HSTS.  
• If the code runs in Node.js, disable console logs in production or pipe them to a secure, access-controlled store.  
• Use Content-Security-Policy and Subresource-Integrity so malicious script can’t hijack the PAN.  
• Rotate API keys and keep them in a vault/secret manager—not a `.env` file committed to VCS.  
• Unit-test validation logic and integration-test API calls with mocks to avoid real charges during CI.

By addressing the items above you eliminate clear-text exposure, comply with PCI rules, improve reliability, and make the function easier to test and maintain.
